# Three properties of every code point in Unicode 17.0.0 that IDNA's checks
# read and `unicode` does not offer: the bidirectional class (RFC 5893), and
# the joining type and the canonical combining class (RFC 5892, Appendix A).
# `lookup.hero` reads it.
#
# The table is data, made and not typed: a one-off program read the Unicode
# Character Database 17.0.0 of https://www.unicode.org/Public/17.0.0/ucd/ on
# 2026-11-17, `extracted/DerivedBidiClass.txt` (SHA-256
# 4867b4b7f0731ed1...), `extracted/DerivedJoiningType.txt`
# (f39ebe974825d673...) and `extracted/DerivedCombiningClass.txt`
# (191463abfbd20270...), their `@missing` defaults included, held the
# bidirectional and combining classes to Python 3.15.2's `unicodedata`
# (17.0.0) for every assigned code point, reduced them to runs, held every
# lookup to the files for each of the 1,114,112 code points, and wrote this
# module whole. Nothing here is edited by hand but the test at its end, which
# reads the packing and was written again with it. Until 2026-11-20 it held a
# fourth property, NFC_Quick_Check, which stood in for a normalization to NFC;
# `unicode/normalize` makes that normalization since the coherence review's
# D17, and the table was made again without it, by a program that first made
# the 1,904 runs of 2026-11-17 again value for value from the same four files
# (`DerivedNormalizationProps.txt`, 71fd6a206a2c0cdd..., the fourth), then the
# 1,716 runs below, every code point's lookup held to the files.

# The runs of one set of the three properties, each `first << 18 | bidi << 11
# | joining << 8 | combining`. The code points from `first` up to the next
# run's share them. `bidi` is 0 L, 1 R, 2 AL, 3 AN, 4 EN, 5 ES, 6 CS, 7 ET, 8
# ON, 9 BN, 10 NSM, and 11 for every class RFC 5893 does not name; `joining`
# is 0 for none (U, and C, which RFC 5892 does not read), 1 L, 2 D, 3 R, 4 T;
# `combining` is the canonical combining class, 0 to 254, 9 being a virama.
# The first run begins at 0 and the runs ascend.
constant RUNS: [i64]
